# Line endings, settled once so they cannot drift again.
#
# `text=auto` means: git decides what is text, stores every text file with LF in the object
# database, and checks it out in whatever the platform wants — CRLF here, because `core.autocrlf`
# is true. Nothing about editing changes; what changes is that storage stops depending on who
# staged the file and with which client.
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# Two stylesheets were committed with CRLF *in the blob* while the other 458 text files in the repo
# held LF. Nobody noticed until a two-line comment fix rewrote one of them through a byte-level
# path, git normalised it on staging, and a 128-line change arrived as a 1670-line diff — which
# destroyed `git blame` on the file it touched (D-152).
#
# The churn was invisible in review precisely because it was *correct*: the commit brought those
# files into line with the repo. What was missing was anything that said what the line was.
#
# Files git detects as binary are unaffected — `apps/worker/test/rasterize.test.ts` embeds a real
# `Exif\0\0` header, and a NUL byte is how git decides a file is not text.
* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test data whose *bytes* are compared, pinned to LF in the working tree as well as in the object
# database.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `text=auto` above is right for everything a person edits: stored LF, checked out however the
# platform likes. It is wrong for a fixture a test reads line by line and compares as strings,
# because the checkout is then part of the test input.
#
# `fixtures/catalogues/*.txt` is one path per line, and it went green on the machine that wrote
# the files and red on the very next checkout of the same commit: stored LF, checked out CRLF,
# so every path arrived as `/shop/rt/\r`. Three assertions failed comparing paths, and the
# failure pointed at the GLP-1 rule — which was matching perfectly. The byte that caused it
# appears in no diff, no error message and no editor.
#
# `eol=lf` fixes the checkout. It is not the whole fix and is not trusted as one: `readCatalogue`
# in `packages/engine/test/glp1Patterns.test.ts` trims every line, so a fixture arriving CRLF
# through some other path still cannot decide whether a suite passes. Two independent guards,
# because this one lives in a file nobody reads until it has already gone wrong.
fixtures/catalogues/*.txt eol=lf
